#!/bin/bash
# e1-hook/run-floor: the floor (PLAN.md, A5), in a safe boot only, as root:
#
#   pkexec /home/corey/src/sleep-drain/e1-hook/run-floor [--quick] [--quiet | --m1 | --m1-observe | --m1-late | --m1-alarm | --m1-hold | --m1-hold-nowatch | --m1-pmp | --m1-pmp-idle | --m1-position | --m1-sio-pmp | --m1-disp-off | --m1-macos-end | --m1-sio-stop | --m1-power-words | --m1-recfg-bite | --m1-wd2-bite | --m1-cluster-ack | --m1-fabric-up | --m1-mark | --m1-gates | --m1-sio-down | --m1-pcie-down | --m1-sio-steps | --m1-a2-pre | --m1-acc-last]
#
# --quick, ahead of the recipe, makes the reading 1 minute instead of 10, with no need to wait
# 20 minutes off the charger: for a run whose question is how it ends. If one ever goes dark for
# good, repeat it without --quick before trusting a drain number from it.
#
# THIS IS ONE WAY: THE MAC NEVER WAKES FROM IT. It loads e1_hook with
# replay=15 oneway=1, so the next suspend's prepare_late runs maralcbr's
# replay at its floor setting, bits 0-3 (e1_hook.c says what: the keys, the
# PMP words, the 74 post-slpa writes, then every CPU powered down for good),
# then takes the 10-minute reading, saves it and suspends, as V2 and E1a did
# (sleep-drain-reading take --label floor --then suspend), and waits for that
# suspend. --quiet (E2's first rung, PLAN.md) adds quiet=1: the same, but
# without maralcbr's 500 ms and its two reads after the post-slpa writes, so
# the commit is the last thing the replay touches before the CPUs power down;
# its reading's label is floor-quiet. --m1 (E2's M1 recipe, PLAN.md) adds m1=1
# to --quiet's: the other CPUs parked before the post-slpa writes and stopped
# one by one before the commit, every interrupt masked, the AP wake mask, boot
# stage 0x50 and breadcrumbs in the PMU (e1_hook.c says what); its reading's
# label is floor-m1. --m1-observe (E2's M1 run 2) adds observe=1 to --m1's:
# CPU7 stays up through the commit, watches CPU0 and the E complex power down,
# reads the watchdogs and records it all in the same breadcrumbs before it
# stops; its label is floor-m1-observe, and what's said of --m1 below holds
# for it too. --m1-late (E2's M1 run 3) adds pmgr_late=1 to --m1-observe's:
# the table's PMGR quiesce goes out after slpa, in its place, with macOS's
# waits, and the breadcrumbs say which waits timed out and when EACC and
# PACC0 finished powering down; its label is floor-m1-late, and what's said
# of --m1 and --m1-observe below holds for it too. --m1-alarm (E2's M1 run 4)
# is --m1 with pmgr_late=1 and alarm=1: the PMGR quiesce as in --m1-late, no
# observer (CPU7 goes down before the commit, as in --m1, so no CPU7 record),
# and the PMU's RTC alarm, for an hour after the commit, written between the
# commit's first write and the other two; its label is floor-m1-alarm, and
# what's said of --m1 below holds for it too. --m1-hold (E2's hold rung,
# PLAN.md) is --m1-alarm with hold=1: each other CPU writes its own stop bit
# in step 6 and holds, awake and spinning, through the commit (modeled on
# XNU's H13 global sleep, whose secondaries stall in WFE); after the third
# commit CPU0 lets go, they power down, and CPU0 reads them off and records
# that, then takes its own deep WFI last; its label is
# floor-m1-hold. --m1-hold-nowatch adds hold_nowatch=1: CPU0 doesn't read
# them, the run to make once a watched one comes back clean; its label is
# floor-m1-hold-nowatch. --m1-pmp (E2's PMP rung, PLAN.md rung 3) is --m1-hold
# with pmp_out=1: right after slpa, while interrupts are still on, the PMP's idle
# pair (SetAPPower 0x201 then SetIOPPower 0x201, once each), and if its ASC then
# reads idle, its CPU stopped with one write (RUN, bit 4 of 0x28ec00044); its
# label is floor-m1-pmp. --m1-pmp-idle adds pmp_stop=0, the idle pair only; its
# label is floor-m1-pmp-idle. --m1-position (E2's position read, PLAN.md) is
# --m1-hold with position=1: after reading the others down CPU0 stays up, every
# exception still masked, and samples where the engine is, about 1 s and about 25
# s after the third commit, and records each sample in the PMU's breadcrumbs. It
# reads only, and writes no PMU byte --m1-hold doesn't. pkexec
# /home/corey/src/sleep-drain/e1-hook/recfg-peek --position must have read every
# word awake first, in this boot, and this refuses without its mark in /run; its
# label is floor-m1-position. --m1-sio-pmp (E2's combined rung, PLAN.md) is --m1-pmp
# with position=1 and sio_on=1: right before the table's SIO_CPU word, ps_sio, UART_P
# and SPI_P on (new PMGR writes, Corey's OK of 2026-11-09); it also withdraws the PMP's
# display report requests (sleep-drain-pmp release 0xc 0xd), and takes one position
# sample at about 1 s, CPU0 then going down at once. A hang in those raises is dark at
# stage 0x90 (0x10 with bit 7). No awake pass needed; its label is floor-m1-sio-pmp.
# --m1-disp-off (E2's display rung, PLAN.md) adds disp_off=1 to that: before noirq, both DCPs'
# firmware shut down through RTKit and their CPUs stopped, then after slpa their domains off
# (new RTKit messages, ASC writes and PMGR writes, Corey's OK of 2026-11-09). A hang there is
# dark at stage 0x02 or 0x84, and a suspend that then fails has no display to show NO FLOOR on
# (its log has it). Its label is floor-m1-disp-off. --m1-macos-end adds one_commit=1 and
# smc_idle=1 to that: the commit written once, and after K4 the AP idle notice to the SMC (a new
# RTKit message, Corey's OK of 2026-11-09). Its label is floor-m1-macos-end. --m1-sio-stop is that
# with SIO's firmware stopped before noirq (its idle pair, and if that returns 0, RUN cleared: Corey's
# OK of 2026-11-09) and hold_nowatch=1 in place of position=1, so CPU0 goes straight to its deep WFI
# after the commit and there's no sample. Its label is floor-m1-sio-stop. --m1-power-words is that with
# ps_words=1: macOS's remaining 20 power words at the end of step 5, after the table (new PMGR writes, Corey's
# OK of 2026-11-09). A hang in them is dark at stage 0xa8. Its label is floor-m1-power-words.
# --m1-recfg-bite (the feasibility run for instrumenting the engine: PLAN.md; RESEARCH.md, "A different
# approach") is that with one word of the engine's reconfig SRAM changed first: A2's WD2 bite, 30 s to 45 s
# (e1-hook/recfg-bite: a read-only check, saved, then the one write, saved; Corey's OK of 2026-11-09, and of
# 2026-11-10 for making it as one 64-bit store, run 13's 32-bit one having been dropped), and
# recfg_bite=1, which has CPU0, once it has let the others go after the commit, read WD2 and PHASE and record
# them (stage 0xc7, or 0xc6 with an exception pending). If the word can't be changed, that's the result:
# nothing is loaded and nothing replays. A write that faults restarts the Mac about 10 s later (kernel.panic,
# below), with the check's record saved. Nothing puts the old value back by itself: the OK is for one write,
# so once it's made the word reads 45 s on every way out, and this says so (recfg-bite --restore, by hand
# in a safe boot, writes 30 s again). Its reset, if the engine ran the changed word and A2 is what stalls,
# comes about 45 s after the screen goes dark, not 30. Its label is floor-m1-recfg-bite.
# --m1-wd2-bite (PLAN.md, "The order after run 14"; RESEARCH.md, "M1 run 14") is --m1-power-words' with the
# reset's time moved, without the SRAM, which dropped both of recfg-bite's stores: WD2's bite register,
# 0x2922b0024, written to 120 s (Corey's OK of 2026-11-10 for that register and value, twice). First, before
# anything is loaded, e1-hook/wd2-bite makes that store once from user space, awake, where WD2 isn't armed (a
# read-only check, saved, then the store, saved): the control runs 13 and 14 lacked, since nothing had shown
# that a store through /dev/mem changes any register here. The run goes on whether it took or not, and stops,
# with nothing loaded, if WD2 isn't as it is awake or wd2-bite fails (a save that fails among that). wd2-bite
# makes that store once in a boot: run again in the same boot, it gives the first one's result. Then
# wd2_bite=1: CPU0, once it has let the others go after the commit and
# has seen WD2 armed, writes the bite once, reads WD2 back and records it (stage 0xd7, or 0xd6 with an
# exception pending). WD2 stays armed and only its timeout changes, so its reset, if the entry is stuck as
# before, comes about 120 s after the screen goes dark, not 30: for this run, "dark for good" below starts
# at 3 minutes, not 2. A reset at about 30 s, or no reset, would each be news (PLAN.md has the readings).
# Its label is floor-m1-wd2-bite.
# --m1-cluster-ack (PLAN.md, "The order after the sweep of 2026-11-10"; RESEARCH.md, "Related work, swept
# 2026-11-10") is --m1-wd2-bite's, all of it, wd2-bite's store and the 120 s included, with three of the
# table's values changed: cluster_ack=1 writes the soc-cluster words of ANE0, AFR and AVEMSR (0x28e09800c,
# 0x28e098014, 0x28e098020) as 0x20, not macOS's 0: target 0, and a 1 to the cluster's "up done" flag, which
# is how such a flag is cleared (Corey's OK of 2026-11-10 for those three words and that value). Under macOS
# those clusters are off and acked long before the sleep; here they're up with the flag set, and the plain 0
# is thought to leave it (inferred: no run has read the words after it). CPU0 then waits up to 10 ms for each
# of the four cluster words, DISPLAY's too, to read 0, and records whose flag stayed and whether anything
# else was left in a word (m1-crumbs: 0x6006's bits 2-5 and 0x6014's bits 4 and 5). The run goes on either
# way, and it ends as --m1-wd2-bite's does: a reset about 120 s after the screen goes dark, or "dark for
# good" from 3 minutes. What a reset says about the flags depends on that record: only if every word read 0
# does it say the flags weren't what stalls the entry, or not alone (PLAN.md). Its label is
# floor-m1-cluster-ack.
# --m1-fabric-up (PLAN.md, "The order after run 16"; RESEARCH.md, "After run 16") is --m1-cluster-ack's, all
# of it, with one write more: fabric_up=1 puts the fabric's p-state word, 0x28e20c000, back to 3, its awake
# value, at the start of the replay's table (Corey's OK of 2026-11-10 for that word and value). This
# kernel lowers it to 1 at every suspend, where macOS has 3 (inferred), and the engine's own first step
# then asks for 2 with no timeout. m1-crumbs says what the word read before the write, whether it read
# 0x333 after it and whether it still did once the clocks were parked (0x6007, at the run's own stages:
# 0xd2, then 0xd3 or 0xd1). It ends as --m1-wd2-bite's does: a reset about 120 s after the screen goes
# dark, or "dark for good" from 3 minutes. Its label is floor-m1-fabric-up.
# --m1-mark (the same sections) is --m1-fabric-up's with a marker in WD1, the kernel's own watchdog, that
# says how far the engine's second sequence, B2, gets once every core is down, which nothing has seen.
# wd1_mark=1 sets it inside the replay, a moment before the commit, where the kernel has already stopped
# WD1: its bite := 150 s, its count := 60 s, then armed (0x2922b0014, 0x2922b0010, 0x2922b001c; shown to
# Corey on 2026-11-10, who answered with the standing go-ahead to build new steps). Nothing is armed while
# the Mac is awake. B2 zeroes WD1's count near its end, so the time of this run's restart is a reading of
# how far B2 got, which m1-crumbs makes, and only where the record bears it out (the marker stood, the
# run's last stage landed, WD2 was as the reading needs it):
#   - a restart about 90 s after the screen goes dark fits WD1 with its count never zeroed: B2 didn't
#     get that far.
#   - a restart about 120 s after fits WD2, as in runs 15 and 16: B2 got past its power-down.
#   - a restart about 150 s after or later, or dark for good, fits a completed entry. For this run "dark
#     for good" starts at 4 minutes, and the steps below for a dark Mac apply.
# One thing changes for every stage of the replay from step 4 on: a hang before the commit, which the
# notes above call dark until the power key, now ends in WD1's restart about 90 s later. m1-crumbs'
# stage tells such a run from one that committed. Its label is floor-m1-mark.
# --m1-gates (PLAN.md, "The order after the look"; RESEARCH.md, "The gate test's run") is --m1-mark's, all of it,
# with reads and no write more: gates_mark=1 has CPU0, right before the commit, read the six gate words the engine's
# B2 polls and the fabric lane above SIO, in the state the recipe leaves, which nothing has read them in, and leave
# their bits in one byte of the PMU (0x6016, in place of the alarm's result; e1-hook/gates_mark.h). m1-crumbs says
# what each gate read, and for 0x28e5a0050, the gate B2 polls at its op 140, what that makes the next step. The run's
# stages are its own from step 8 on (0xcb, then 0xd5 or 0xd4), and it ends as --m1-mark's does, the same three
# readings of the restart's time included. One ending is new: a read that faults or never returns leaves stage 0xcb
# with no gate byte, and a restart about 10 s (a panic) or 90 s (WD1's marker) after the screen goes dark. Its label
# is floor-m1-gates.
# --m1-sio-down (PLAN.md, "The order after the look"; e1-hook/sio_down.h) is --m1-gates', with one thing in place of
# its reads, and this one writes: sio_down=1 has CPU0, right before the commit, write 0 to the 43 power words of SIO's
# subtree (0x28e580180 to 0x28e5802d0) in the order the engine's B2 does, poll each as B2 polls it, poll the two gates B2
# then waits for (0x28e5a0064, and 0x28e5a0050 at its op 140), leave the result in the PMU's 0x6016, write all 43 back as
# found, and leave whether they read as found. New writes: those 43 words, each to 0 and back. SIO's firmware is
# stopped by then, as in every run since --m1-sio-stop. m1-crumbs says whether the gate closed and what that makes the
# next step, or which word stayed up (the byte's second form, since the first run: sio_down.h). If the gate reads
# closed before the first write, nothing is written. The run's stages are its own from step 8 on (0xbd, then 0xbf
# or 0xbe), and it ends as --m1-gates' does, the same three readings of the restart's time included, as long as the
# byte says the 43 were put back. A run that stops inside the switch-off leaves stage 0xbd with no byte, or a byte
# without "written back", and a restart about 10 s (a panic) or 90 s (WD1's marker) after the screen goes dark. Its
# label is floor-m1-sio-down.
# --m1-pcie-down (PLAN.md, "The order after the look"; e1-hook/pcie_down.h) is --m1-gates', with one thing in place of
# its reads, and this one writes: pcie_down=1 has CPU0, right before the commit, write 0 to the three PCIe power words
# the engine's B2 switches off and polls (APCIE_ST_SYS, APCIE_ST1_SYS, APCIE_GP_SYS: 0x28e0802e8, 0x28e0802f0,
# 0x28e080320), poll each as B2 polls it, write 0 to APCIE_ST and APCIE_GP (0x28e080148, 0x28e080150) as B2 does next,
# poll the gate B2 then waits for (0x28e09c34c), and leave the result in the PMU's 0x6016. New writes: those five words,
# each to 0. Nothing is written back: the commit finds them off, so the engine's own 0 meets words that are off already,
# and a restart at about 120 s in place of 90 s would say B2 got past its power-down. No PCIe register is touched. The
# run's stages are its own from step 8 on (0x9d, then 0x9f or 0x9e), and it ends as --m1-gates' does. A run that stops
# inside the switch-off leaves stage 0x9d with no byte, or with its first send, and a restart about 10 s (a panic) or
# 90 s (WD1's marker) after the screen goes dark. Its label is floor-m1-pcie-down.
# --m1-sio-steps (PLAN.md, "The order after the look"; e1-hook/sio_steps.h) is --m1-gates', with one thing in place of
# its reads, and this one writes: sio_steps=1 asks what lets the gate 0x28e5a0050 close, which --m1-sio-down's
# switch-off left open twice. CPU0, right before the commit, makes that switch-off of SIO's subtree with the engine's
# cycle of GPIO's power word inside it (0x28e080120 := 0x8000000f before, := 0 after the first 38 writes) and polls the
# gate; if it is still open, writes the 22 words the engine's B2 writes first (0x28e03801c := 0x80101100, 1 to
# 0x28e0a0004-1c and to 0x28e5b0004-38) and polls it again; then writes the 43 back as found, with up to 100 ms each.
# New writes: the two to GPIO's word and the 22, which stay as the engine itself would leave them. The result is in
# the PMU's 0x6016. The run's stages are its own from step 8 on (0x95, then 0x97 or 0x96), and it ends as --m1-gates'
# does. A run that stops inside leaves stage 0x95 and a byte that says how far it got, and a restart about 10 s (a
# panic) or 90 s (WD1's marker) after the screen goes dark. Its label is floor-m1-sio-steps.
# --m1-a2-pre (PLAN.md, "The order after the look"; e1-hook/a2_pre.h) is --m1-gates', with one thing in place of its
# reads, and this one writes: a2_pre=1 has CPU0, right before the commit, make the three moves the engine's A2 makes by
# itself after its first handshake, in A2's order, each with up to 100 ms for the condition A2 waits on with no bound:
# 2 to DCS's p-state word (0x28e20c800), 2 to the fabric's (0x28e20c000) and 0 to 0x28e20c400. Before them it reads
# the five clock muxes the table parked, which nothing had read after their write. New writes: those three words, with
# values no run has written. Nothing is written back: the commit finds them made, so the engine's own writes meet words
# that are already there, and a restart at about 120 s in place of 90 s would say B2 got past its power-down. The
# result is in the PMU's 0x6016. The run's stages are its own from step 8 on (0x99, then 0x9b or 0x9a), and it ends as
# --m1-gates' does. A run that stops inside a move leaves stage 0x99 and a byte that stops short of that move, and a
# restart about 10 s (a panic) or 90 s (WD1's marker) after the screen goes dark. Its label is floor-m1-a2-pre.
# --m1-acc-last (PLAN.md, "The order after the look"; e1-hook/acc_last.h) is --m1-gates', with the place of three of
# the table's writes changed and one thing in place of its reads: acc_last=1 has step 5 leave out the soc-cluster
# words of the three CPU complexes (0x28e098000 := 0, 0x28e098004 := 0x20, 0x28e098008 := 0x20, the table's entries
# 52 to 54) and has CPU0 write them last, right before the commit, so that only the commit, the alarm, the release and
# the last record come between them and the cores' final wait. The time from before the first of them to after the
# run's last stage goes to the PMU's 0x6016 as a code, in one more command after that stage. In step 8 it reads the
# five clock muxes the table parked, one bit each. No new address and no new value. The run's stages are its own from
# step 8 on (0x91, then 0x93 or 0x92), and it ends as --m1-gates' does: a restart at about 120 s in place of 90 s, or
# none, would say B2 got past its power-down. Its label is floor-m1-acc-last.
# What's said of --m1, --m1-alarm and --m1-hold
# below holds for all of them. It ends one of three ways:
#   - A reset: about 30 s after the screen goes dark, the Mac restarts by
#     itself into the regular boot. That's most likely the S2R entry's
#     watchdog, WD2 (RESEARCH.md, "The floor"): the entry didn't complete.
#     After --m1-observe and --m1-late, m1-crumbs says which watchdogs were
#     armed at CPU7's read after its polls (at most about 1.5 s after the
#     commit, likely a few ms) and when the first would bite;
#     if it was WD1, armed again, the entry may have completed before it.
#     In the regular boot, pkexec /usr/local/bin/sleep-drain-safe collect;
#     BACC dates it.
#     After --m1, also, before anything else touches the PMU:
#       /home/corey/src/sleep-drain/e1-hook/m1-crumbs, for a photo or a copy,
#       then pkexec /home/corey/src/sleep-drain/e1-hook/m1-crumbs --clear
#       (after --m1-alarm, --m1-hold, --m1-position, --m1-sio-pmp, --m1-disp-off, --m1-macos-end, --m1-sio-stop, --m1-power-words, --m1-recfg-bite, --m1-wd2-bite, --m1-cluster-ack, --m1-fabric-up, --m1-mark, --m1-gates, --m1-sio-down, --m1-pcie-down, --m1-sio-steps, --m1-a2-pre and --m1-acc-last, --clear also disarms the alarm the run armed, 0x1401
#       back to 0x01, so that it can't power the Mac on an hour later; every
#       m1 run refuses to load while it reads armed).
#     After --m1-position, also, in that same boot, once m1-crumbs and collect
#     are done (the next restart would lose what the reset left):
#       pkexec /home/corey/src/sleep-drain/e1-hook/recfg-peek --position-after
#     After --m1-recfg-bite, also, once m1-crumbs and collect are done (and after the dark ending's
#     power key too):
#       pkexec /home/corey/src/sleep-drain/e1-hook/recfg-bite --after
#     (does A2's bite word read 30 s again, or did the 45 s survive the restart? If it reads 45 s: no
#     other one-way recipe, and no macOS, before recfg-bite --restore in the next safe boot, Corey's call)
#   - Dark for good: the screen goes dark, if it isn't already, and it and
#     the keyboard (dark since the reading began) stay dark past 2 minutes.
#     Nothing wakes the Mac from it. If that's new (it's a candidate for a
#     completed entry), leave it at least 30 minutes, then the steps below.
#     After --m1-alarm, --m1-hold, --m1-position, --m1-sio-pmp, --m1-disp-off, --m1-macos-end, --m1-sio-stop, --m1-power-words, --m1-recfg-bite, --m1-wd2-bite, --m1-cluster-ack, --m1-fabric-up, --m1-mark, --m1-gates, --m1-sio-down, --m1-pcie-down, --m1-sio-steps, --m1-a2-pre or --m1-acc-last, do them before about 55 minutes: the alarm fires 60
#     minutes after the commit, and if it wakes the SoC, the wake lands in
#     m1n1 with no way back and the Mac hangs, at an unknown draw, until the
#     power key. If it's dark past that, expect that and end it the same way.
#       1. Press the power key once, briefly, and wait 30 s. If the screen
#          comes back, it's the last ending, below.
#       2. If it stays dark, hold the key until the Mac is off (about 10 s;
#          the screen gives no cue), let go, and press it once more straight
#          away, so that BACC dates the end. Then, in the next boot:
#            pkexec bash -c '/usr/local/bin/sleep-drain-safe collect &&
#                            /usr/local/bin/sleep-drain-reading take --label floor'
#          and after run-floor --quiet, the same with --label floor-quiet.
#          After --m1: m1-crumbs first, as above, then the same with --label
#          floor-m1 (floor-m1-observe after --m1-observe, floor-m1-late
#          after --m1-late, floor-m1-alarm after --m1-alarm, floor-m1-hold
#          and floor-m1-hold-nowatch after those, floor-m1-pmp and
#          floor-m1-pmp-idle after --m1-pmp and --m1-pmp-idle, floor-m1-position
#          after --m1-position, floor-m1-sio-pmp after --m1-sio-pmp, floor-m1-disp-off after --m1-disp-off, floor-m1-macos-end after --m1-macos-end, floor-m1-sio-stop after --m1-sio-stop, floor-m1-power-words after --m1-power-words, floor-m1-recfg-bite after --m1-recfg-bite: --clear before
#          the reading, since a PMU write can't change BQCC). After --m1-position,
#          collect on its own first, then, before the reading, recfg-peek
#          --position-after as above. --m1 can also hang
#          dark without completing the entry: DeepIdle's R20 did, on an M2
#          Pro, and its S2R watchdog never reset it. Only that reading's BQCC tells the two apart, and m1-crumbs
#          says how far it got, at least. So for --m1, be ready to end it
#          this way.
#   - No floor: the keyboard lights up, full, and this prints "NO FLOOR"
#     with what happened (unlock the screen to see it), and tries to save it.
#     Photograph it, then restart. That happens when the suspend
#     fails, when the replay stops short (e1_hook then fails the suspend, so
#     that the Mac wakes with its log), or when no suspend begins within
#     120 s. With e1_hook loaded, nothing else can put the Mac to sleep: only
#     the first suspend after its load can replay, and any suspend that
#     doesn't replay fails. So dark always means the floor.
#
# A fault in the replay (an oops, or the SError a bad write raises) would
# leave the Mac hung and dark, as if it were in the floor. So this sets
# kernel.panic=10 and kernel.panic_on_oops=1 before it loads the module: a
# fault then restarts the Mac 10 s later (into the regular boot), which BACC
# dates. They stay set until the restart.
#
# Until the reading has asked for the suspend, any other way out (a failed
# reading or save, a closed terminal, a signal, a broken stdout) unloads the
# module again, so nothing stays armed without its saved reading. After that,
# a way out calls the replay off instead: an unload then would let the
# suspend already asked for sleep as a plain s2idle, dark like the floor.
# With --m1-recfg-bite, either way leaves A2's bite word at 45 s, and says so.
